window.COURSE={"pages":[{"key":"home","file":"java/index.html","name":"Home","short":"Home","band":"","sections":[]},{"key":"l01","file":"java/lessons/01-strings.html","name":"Lesson 01 · What is a String?","short":"L01","band":"str","sections":[{"id":"st-what","n":"01","t":"What is a String?"},{"id":"st-create","n":"02","t":"Three ways to create one"},{"id":"st-memory","n":"03","t":"Where a String lives: stack, heap, pool"},{"id":"st-pool","n":"04","t":"The String Constant Pool (SCP)"},{"id":"st-new","n":"05","t":"What new String(\"x\") really does"},{"id":"st-compare","n":"06","t":"== vs equals"},{"id":"st-immutable","n":"07","t":"Why a String can never change"},{"id":"st-concat","n":"08","t":"Compile time vs run time"},{"id":"st-qa","n":"09","t":"10 interview questions"},{"id":"st-review","n":"10","t":"Review"}]},{"key":"l02","file":"java/lessons/02-classes-objects.html","name":"Lesson 02 · What is a Class and an Object?","short":"L02","band":"cls","sections":[{"id":"co-what","n":"01","t":"What is a class? What is an object?"},{"id":"co-anatomy","n":"02","t":"Inside a class: fields, constructors, methods"},{"id":"co-new","n":"03","t":"What new really does"},{"id":"co-memory","n":"04","t":"Where objects live: stack, heap, Metaspace"},{"id":"co-ctor","n":"05","t":"Constructors and this"},{"id":"co-static","n":"06","t":"static vs instance: the class's data vs each object's data"},{"id":"co-print","n":"07","t":"Printing and comparing objects"},{"id":"co-mistakes","n":"08","t":"Common mistakes"},{"id":"co-qa","n":"09","t":"10 interview questions"},{"id":"co-review","n":"10","t":"Review"}]},{"key":"pg","file":"java/playground.html","name":"Playground","short":"PG","band":"","sections":[]}],"qa":[{"k":"st-qa:01","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is a String? Is it a primitive?","a":"An object of the <code>final</code> class <code>java.lang.String</code>: an immutable sequence of characters stored in a <code>byte[]</code>. It is not a primitive, but the language gives it special support (literals, <code>+</code>, <code>switch</code>)."},{"k":"st-qa:02","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"Where does a String live: stack or heap?","a":"The <b>variable</b> is a reference in a stack frame (or a field in another object). The String object and its <code>byte[]</code> are always on the heap. Pooled Strings are heap objects too, since Java 7."},{"k":"st-qa:03","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is the String Constant Pool? Where is it?","a":"A table holding one shared copy of each literal (and of each interned String). It moved from PermGen to the main heap in Java 7, so its Strings can be garbage-collected."},{"k":"st-qa:04","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is the difference between == and equals()?","a":"<code>==</code> compares references and <code>equals()</code> compares characters. Always use <code>equals</code> for content, literal first for null safety."},{"k":"st-qa:05","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"How many objects does <code>new String(\"abc\")</code> create?","a":"One or two: the pooled literal (if it isn't already there) plus one new heap object. The two share one <code>byte[]</code>."},{"k":"st-qa:06","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What does intern() do?","a":"It returns the canonical pooled copy with the same content, adding this object's reference to the pool if the text is missing. Since Java 7 nothing is copied."},{"k":"st-qa:07","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"Why is String immutable?","a":"Pool sharing is safe, the <code>hashCode</code> can be cached for fast HashMap keys, it is thread-safe with no locks, checked values can't change (security), and class loading is reliable."},{"k":"st-qa:08","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"Why is <code>\"a\" + \"b\" == \"ab\"</code> true, but <code>a + \"b\" == \"ab\"</code> false?","a":"Literal concatenation is a compile-time constant, so it is pooled. With a non-final variable, the String is built at run time as a new object. A <code>final</code> variable makes the result <code>true</code> again."},{"k":"st-qa:09","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"Is a String passed by reference?","a":"Java is always pass-by-value: the method gets a copy of the <b>reference</b>. It can read the same object, but reassigning the parameter does not touch the caller's variable, and the String itself can't change anyway."},{"k":"st-qa:10","l":"L01","sec":"st-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"Can String be extended? Why is it final?","a":"No. Making it final protects immutability and security (a subclass could otherwise override methods and lie about its content), and it allows JVM optimisations."},{"k":"co-qa:01","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is a class, and what is an object?","a":"A <b>class</b> is a blueprint (a user-defined type) that declares state (fields) and behaviour (methods). An <b>object</b> is an instance of that class created at run time with <code>new</code>; it lives on the heap and has its own copy of the instance fields."},{"k":"co-qa:02","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is the difference between a class and an object?","a":"A class is a logical template, written once and loaded once; it takes no heap memory for instance data. An object is a physical instance: you can create many from one class, each on the heap with its own state, its own identity, and the class's behaviour."},{"k":"co-qa:03","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What happens when you write <code>new Car(\"Swift\")</code>?","a":"The JVM (loads the class if needed and) allocates memory on the heap with every field at its default value, runs the constructor with <code>this</code> = the new object, and returns a reference to it, which is stored in the variable."},{"k":"co-qa:04","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"Where are objects stored? Where are references stored?","a":"Objects always live on the heap. A local reference variable lives in its method's stack frame; a reference held in a field lives inside the object that owns the field (on the heap). Class metadata and method code are in Metaspace."},{"k":"co-qa:05","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"Does <code>Car c = a;</code> create a new object?","a":"No. It copies the <b>reference</b>, so <code>a</code> and <code>c</code> point to the same object and <code>a == c</code> is true. A change through one is visible through the other."},{"k":"co-qa:06","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What are the default values of fields? Do local variables get them?","a":"Numbers 0 / 0.0, <code>boolean</code> false, <code>char</code> character 0, references <code>null</code>. Local variables get no default; reading one before assigning it is a compile error."},{"k":"co-qa:07","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is a constructor? How is it different from a method?","a":"Code that initialises a new object: it has the class's name, no return type, runs once per object inside <code>new</code>, and is not inherited. A method has a return type (or <code>void</code>), any name, and can be called any number of times on an existing object."},{"k":"co-qa:08","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is <code>this</code>?","a":"A reference to the current object, available in constructors and instance methods. Used to reach a field shadowed by a parameter (<code>this.model = model</code>), to call another constructor (<code>this(…)</code>), or to pass the current object somewhere. Static methods have no <code>this</code>."},{"k":"co-qa:09","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"What is the difference between static and instance members?","a":"Instance members belong to each object (one copy per object, need an object to use). Static members belong to the class (one shared copy, used via the class name, exist before any object). A static method has no <code>this</code>, so it can't use instance fields directly."},{"k":"co-qa:10","l":"L02","sec":"co-qa","st":"10 interview questions","q":"In how many ways can you create an object in Java?","a":"The usual answer is five: <code>new</code>; reflection (<code>Car.class.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance()</code>); <code>clone()</code>; deserialization; and factory methods such as <code>List.of()</code> or <code>LocalDate.now()</code>, which use one of the others inside. <code>new</code> is the one you use every day."}]};
